---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro'
import Steps from '../../components/Steps.astro'
---

<Guide
  slug="backups"
  title="Backups and recovery"
  description="Take an encrypted backup of your vault, prove you can restore it, and know what each recovery path can and cannot do."
  lede="Your vault exists on one machine. A failed drive is the ordinary way to lose it, and nothing is copied anywhere unless you copy it."
>
  <h2>Taking a backup</h2>
  <Steps steps={[
    'Open <strong>Settings</strong>, then the <strong>Data</strong> tab.',
    'Click <strong>Create backup</strong> and choose where to write the <code>.sanctumbak</code> file.',
    'Give the backup its own password. It can differ from your vault passphrase &mdash; it is what protects this file.',
    'Copy the file somewhere off this machine: another drive, or storage you trust.',
  ]} />

  <p>
    The file contains the whole encrypted database, sealed again under the backup password. Someone
    holding it without that password gets nothing, and a file that has been altered by even one
    byte is refused rather than partly trusted.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="An untested backup is a guess">
    <p>
      The only way to know a backup works is to restore one, and the only safe time to find out is
      before you need it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Restoring replaces the current vault entirely, so do it deliberately &mdash; ideally straight
      after taking a fresh backup, when losing the current state costs nothing.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The three ways back in</h2>
  <p>Each covers a different failure, and knowing which is which matters when something goes wrong.</p>

  <div class="twoUp">
    <article class="tile">
      <h3>You know the passphrase</h3>
      <p>Ordinary unlock. Nothing else needed.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="tile">
      <h3>You lost the passphrase</h3>
      <p>
        The recovery code opens the vault and lets you set a new passphrase. Your data is intact
        &mdash; the code unwraps the same key.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="tile">
      <h3>You lost the machine</h3>
      <p>
        Install Sanctum elsewhere and restore your <code>.sanctumbak</code> with its password. You
        get the vault as of that backup.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="tile tile--warn">
      <h3>You lost passphrase and code</h3>
      <p>
        The vault cannot be opened by anyone, including you. Only a backup and its password help
        here. If neither exists, the data is gone permanently.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h2>Changing your passphrase</h2>
  <p>
    Settings &rarr; Security. Changing it is fast regardless of how much is in the vault, because
    your passphrase never encrypted the records directly &mdash; it wraps a key, and only that
    wrapper is rewritten. Nothing is re-encrypted, so there is no window where a crash could leave
    the vault half-converted.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rotating the recovery code works the same way and requires your current passphrase. The old
    code stops working immediately, so replace your stored copy at the same time.
  </p>

  <h2>Exporting to CSV</h2>
  <p>
    Settings &rarr; Data offers a CSV export, for moving to another tool. It is written with
    permissions restricted to your user account, and Sanctum warns before writing it.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="danger" title="A CSV export is plaintext">
    <p>
      Every password in it is readable by anything that can read the file, and it is no longer
      protected by anything Sanctum does. It also lands in a folder that may be synced to cloud
      storage without you thinking about it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use it to migrate, then delete it &mdash; and remember that a deleted file is often
      recoverable. Never use CSV as a backup format; the <code>.sanctumbak</code> container exists
      for that and is encrypted.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Resetting the vault</h2>
  <p>
    Settings &rarr; Data has a Reset Vault action, which deletes every record along with your
    display name and picture, and returns the app to first-run setup. It requires typing a
    confirmation phrase.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your recovery code will not bring anything back afterwards &mdash; it opens a vault, and there
    will not be one. If there is any chance you want the data later, take a backup first.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="A gap worth knowing about">
    <p>
      Reset Vault lives in Settings, which needs an unlocked vault. If you have forgotten your
      passphrase <em>and</em> your recovery code, you cannot reach it &mdash; the one situation
      where you most want a reset is the one where the button is unreachable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Until that is addressed, the workaround is to delete the vault file directly from
      <code>%APPDATA%\dev.sanctum.vault</code>. Sanctum will start fresh on next launch.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Guide>
